---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import Copy from '../../components/Copy.astro';
import Logo from '../../components/Logo.astro';
import Media from '../../components/landing/Media.astro';
import Lifecycle from '../../components/landing/Lifecycle.astro';
import { u, DEMO, GH } from '../../data/site';

// Same SDK call and 402 body as the handler (rest.go, CheckBudget's reason
// string). Only the dollar amounts are an example. The SDK class keeps its
// old name until the rename lands.
const SAMPLE = `const pal = new PalClient({
  baseUrl: "http://localhost:8080",
  apiKey: process.env.PAL_API_KEY,
});

await pal.agents.create({ name: "triage" });
await pal.budgets.upsert("triage", {
  maxCostUsdPerDay: 25,
  hardFail: true,
});

await pal.runs.create({ agent: "triage", input });`;
const REASON = 'daily spend would reach $25.0318 exceeding limit $25.0000';
const RESPONSE = `{
  "error": "agent budget limit reached: ${REASON}",
  "reason": "${REASON}"
}`;
const TOKEN = /(\/\/.*$)|("[^"]*")|\b(const|new|await|true)\b|\b(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)\b/gm;
const esc = (s: string) => s.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;');
const hl = (src: string) => {
  let out = '', last = 0;
  for (const m of src.matchAll(TOKEN)) {
    const cls = m[1] ? 'c' : m[2] ? 's' : m[3] ? 'k' : 'n';
    out += esc(src.slice(last, m.index)) + `<span class="${cls}">${esc(m[0])}</span>`;
    last = m.index! + m[0].length;
  }
  return out + esc(src.slice(last));
};

const TOC = [['local', 'Laptop to cloud'], ['durable', 'Crash-resume'], ['sandbox', 'Sandboxes'], ['invoke', 'Invoke'], ['budgets', 'Budgets'], ['telemetry', 'Telemetry'], ['resilience', 'Resilience'], ['security', 'Security']];
const SPANS = [
  { n: 'POST /v1/runs', l: 0, w: 100, k: 'req' },
  { n: 'budget check', l: 2, w: 6, k: 'ok' },
  { n: 'step plan · llm', l: 9, w: 34, k: 'llm' },
  { n: 'step fetch · tool (microVM)', l: 44, w: 22, k: 'tool' },
  { n: 'step reply · llm', l: 67, w: 28, k: 'llm' },
  { n: 'receipt', l: 96, w: 3, k: 'ok' },
];
---
<Page path="runtime/" title="PAL Runtime — agents that finish, stay contained, and stay yours" description="PAL Runtime, the open-source runtime for the agents you build: design on your laptop and run the same agent in your cloud, with crash-resume, microVM sandboxes, budgets enforced before the model call, telemetry and signed receipts. Apache-2.0.">
  <section class="hero page-hero rt" aria-labelledby="hero-h">
    <div class="hero-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div data-reveal><Logo variant="runtime" size={24} /></div>
      <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:28px" data-reveal>PAL Runtime · for builders · Apache-2.0</p>
      <h1 id="hero-h" class="display" style="max-width:15ch;margin-top:16px" data-reveal>Agents that finish, stay contained, and <span class="grad-rt">stay yours.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top:24px" data-reveal>An open-source runtime for the agents you build. Write one on your laptop and run the same thing in your cloud: runs that survive a crash, code in a microVM, spend capped before the model is called, and a receipt for every run.</p>
      <div class="actions" style="margin-top:32px" data-reveal>
        <a class="btn btn-rt" href={u('docs/runtime/quickstart/')}>Quickstart</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={GH}>View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page" data-reveal>{TOC.map(([id, l]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{l}</a>)}</nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="local" class="section band" aria-labelledby="local-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="feature">
        <div data-reveal>
          <p class="eyebrow"><b class="rt-k">01</b> · Laptop to cloud</p>
          <h2 id="local-h" class="h2">Design on your laptop. Ship the same agent.</h2>
          <p class="body2">The full stack runs locally with one command, including real Firecracker microVMs on an Apple Silicon Mac. An agent is one <code>agent.yaml</code>; each version is stored as an immutable bundle with a digest, so what you tested is what runs.</p>
          <ul class="facts">
            <li>Point the CLI at another control plane and deploy the same file.</li>
            <li>The data plane runs in your VPC (EKS, GKE, AKS or a plain host) and dials out. No inbound ports.</li>
          </ul>
          <a class="arrow-link" style="margin-top:12px" href={u('docs/runtime/local-microvms/')}>Firecracker on a Mac</a>
        </div>
        <div data-reveal><Media name="runtime-architecture" /></div>
      </div>
      <ol class="ladder" style="margin-top:56px" data-reveal>
        <li><h3>Describe</h3><p><code>pal onboard</code> turns one sentence into a running agent. Or scaffold with <code>pal init</code>.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Iterate</h3><p><code>pal agents dev</code> watches the folder, republishes on save and streams run events.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Gate</h3><p><code>pal test</code> runs the eval suite against your baseline. A regression returns 422 and blocks the deploy.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Deploy</h3><p><code>pal deploy</code> sends the same <code>agent.yaml</code> to whichever control plane you’re logged in to.</p></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:12px">The CLI is <code>pal</code> (<code>lantern</code> still works as an alias). <code>pal doctor</code> checks health, auth, your model provider and a live run.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="durable" class="section" aria-labelledby="durable-h">
    <div class="wrap feature flip">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow"><b class="rt-k">02</b> · Checkpoints and crash-resume</p>
        <h2 id="durable-h" class="h2">A crash doesn’t start the run over.</h2>
        <p class="body2">Every step is written to the run’s journal as it finishes. If a worker dies, a recovery sweep reclaims the run: finished steps come back from the journal and only the unfinished step runs again.</p>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li>The sweep runs every 30 seconds; a lock makes sure one worker resumes a run.</li>
          <li>Side effects carry an idempotency key from run, step and attempt, so a retried model call is billed once.</li>
          <li>Child runs are created and driven by the engine itself, with a depth cap of 5.</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="arrow-link" style="margin-top:12px" href={u('docs/runtime/durable-execution/')}>Durable execution</a>
      </div>
      <div data-reveal><Media name="runtime-durable" /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="sandbox" class="section band" aria-labelledby="sandbox-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow"><b class="rt-k">03</b> · Sandboxes</p>
        <h2 id="sandbox-h" class="h2" style="margin-top:12px">Untrusted code runs in a microVM you can pause, resume and fork.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Firecracker or Kata, never a bare container. Pause snapshots a VM and frees its quota slot; resume brings back the same id. Set an idle timeout and it suspends itself, then wakes on the next exec.</p>
      </div>
      <div data-reveal><Lifecycle /></div>
      <div class="feature" style="margin-top:96px">
        <div data-reveal>
          <h3 class="h2">Nothing leaks.</h3>
          <p class="body2">In broker mode the VM has no network device at all. Its only way out is a broker on the host, which checks each request against the allowlist, adds credentials the guest never sees, and audits every decision.</p>
          <ul class="facts">
            <li>Requests to hosts off the allowlist are refused and audited.</li>
            <li>Injected credentials never enter the VM, its logs or its audit trail.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="small" style="margin-top:12px">Broker mode is opt-in and Firecracker-only<sup class="fn"><a href="#fn-1" id="r-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
          <a class="arrow-link" style="margin-top:4px" href={u('docs/runtime/egress/')}>Egress and secrets</a>
        </div>
        <div data-reveal><Media name="runtime-egress" /></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="invoke" class="section" aria-labelledby="invoke-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow"><b class="rt-k">04</b> · Invoke</p>
        <h2 id="invoke-h" class="h2" style="margin-top:12px">Call it the way your system already works.</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="formats">
        <li data-reveal><h3>Request</h3><code>POST /v1/runs</code><p>Start a run from the SDK, CLI or HTTP, then read its result.</p></li>
        <li data-reveal><h3>Stream</h3><code>GET /v1/runs/&#123;id&#125;/events</code><p>Server-sent events as steps run, end to end with no buffering.</p></li>
        <li data-reveal><h3>Webhook</h3><code>POST /v1/hooks/&#123;id&#125;</code><p>A signed URL per agent, so GitHub, Sentry or any HMAC sender can start runs.</p></li>
        <li data-reveal><h3>Cron</h3><code>POST /v1/schedules</code><p>Cron expressions in the timezone you choose.</p></li>
        <li class="soon" data-reveal><h3>Batch &amp; callbacks<span class="badge">coming soon</span></h3><code>—</code><p>Many inputs in one call, with a callback when they finish.</p></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="actions" style="margin-top:20px">
        <a class="arrow-link" href={u('docs/build/triggers/')}>Webhook triggers</a>
        <a class="arrow-link" href={u('docs/build/scheduling/')}>Scheduling</a>
        <a class="arrow-link" href={u('docs/runtime/streaming/')}>Streaming</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="budgets" class="section band" aria-labelledby="budget-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="feature">
        <div data-reveal>
          <p class="eyebrow"><b class="rt-k">05</b> · Budgets and routing</p>
          <h2 id="budget-h" class="h2">A budget before the first token.</h2>
          <p class="body2">Cap what an agent may spend. Over the cap, the run is refused with HTTP 402 and the model is never called. Code asks for a capability like <code>reasoning-large</code>; the router picks the vendor and fails over when one is down.</p>
        </div>
        <div data-reveal><Media name="runtime-guardrails" /></div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top:48px;align-items:start" data-reveal>
        <div>
          <pre class="code" tabindex="0" aria-label="TypeScript SDK example"><code set:html={hl(SAMPLE)} /></pre>
          <p class="small" style="margin-top:10px">TypeScript shown; Python and Go SDKs cover the same surface.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="response" aria-label="The response when the cap is reached">
          <div class="response-bar"><span class="status-pill">402 Payment Required</span><span>POST /v1/runs</span></div>
          <pre class="code" tabindex="0"><code>{RESPONSE}</code></pre>
          <figcaption class="small" style="padding:0 22px 18px">No model call is made. The fields and wording are the handler’s; the dollar amounts are an example<sup class="fn"><a href="#fn-2" id="r-2">2</a></sup>.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="telemetry" class="section" aria-labelledby="tel-h">
    <div class="wrap feature flip">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow"><b class="rt-k">06</b> · Telemetry</p>
        <h2 id="tel-h" class="h2">Every request and step is a span.</h2>
        <p class="body2">OpenTelemetry traces on every HTTP request, gRPC call and run step, tagged with tenant, run, step and agent version, so one filter finds one run across every service.</p>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li>Prometheus alerts, dashboards and runbooks ship in the repo.</li>
          <li>Each run’s journal streams live and replays later.</li>
          <li>Off by default and free when off: no endpoint, no cost.</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="arrow-link" style="margin-top:12px" href={u('docs/runtime/observability/')}>Observability</a>
      </div>
      <figure class="trace" data-reveal aria-label="An example trace: one run request with a budget check, two model steps, a tool step in a microVM and a receipt">
        <div class="trace-head"><span>trace</span><span class="trace-tags">tenant · run · step · agent_version</span></div>
        <ul>
          {SPANS.map((s) => (
            <li><span class="trace-n">{s.n}</span><span class="trace-track"><i class={`k-${s.k}`} style={`left:${s.l}%;width:${s.w}%`}></i></span></li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <figcaption>Illustration. Span names and widths are an example.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="resilience" class="section band" aria-labelledby="res-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow"><b class="rt-k">07</b> · Resilience</p>
        <h2 id="res-h" class="h2" style="margin-top:12px">Built to keep going, and to fail loudly.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="card" data-reveal><h3 class="h3">Provider failover</h3><p>Four routing strategies (cheap, fast, best, balanced). A rate-limited provider is skipped and the next one answers.</p></div>
        <div class="card" data-reveal><h3 class="h3">Retries that don’t double-bill</h3><p>The same idempotency key rides every retry, so the provider dedups instead of charging twice.</p></div>
        <div class="card" data-reveal><h3 class="h3">Runs get reclaimed</h3><p>A run whose worker vanished is picked up by the recovery sweep within 30 seconds and resumed, not restarted.</p></div>
        <div class="card" data-reveal><h3 class="h3">Heartbeats</h3><p>Each VM’s harness reports every 5 seconds; one silent for 30 is marked stale.</p></div>
        <div class="card" data-reveal><h3 class="h3">No fake success</h3><p>With no model router or VM manager wired, a step fails with a typed error instead of inventing an answer.</p></div>
        <div class="card" data-reveal><h3 class="h3">Refuses unsafe prod</h3><p>In production, services refuse to start without their service token, scheduler or real VM manager.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="security" class="section" aria-labelledby="trust-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow"><b class="rt-k">08</b> · Security</p>
        <h2 id="trust-h" class="h2" style="margin-top:12px">Built for more than one tenant from day one.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="card" data-reveal><h3 class="h3">Tenant isolation</h3><p>Every row carries a tenant, every internal call carries it too, and row-level security policies cover every tenant table<sup class="fn"><a href="#fn-3" id="r-3">3</a></sup>.</p></div>
        <div class="card" data-reveal><h3 class="h3">Trust boundaries</h3><p>Internal gRPC ports accept a tenant only from callers holding the service token.</p></div>
        <div class="card" data-reveal><h3 class="h3">Secrets by reference</h3><p>Code refers to a secret by name; the value is resolved at execution time and never lands in logs, traces or run state.</p></div>
        <div class="card" data-reveal><h3 class="h3">Signed receipts</h3><p>A finished run gets an Ed25519-signed receipt over its journal; anyone can verify it, and any later edit breaks the signature<sup class="fn"><a href="#fn-4" id="r-4">4</a></sup>.</p></div>
        <div class="card" data-reveal><h3 class="h3">Scoped API keys</h3><p>Keys carry scopes, and a key can’t mint another key with more power than it has.</p></div>
        <div class="card" data-reveal><h3 class="h3">Open to audit</h3><p>Apache-2.0, Go and Rust, with tests and linters gating every change in CI.</p></div>
      </div>
      <a class="arrow-link" style="margin-top:20px" href={u('docs/runtime/security/')}>Security model</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section closer closer-grad" aria-labelledby="close-h">
    <div class="wrap" data-reveal>
      <p class="eyebrow">Proof, not promises</p>
      <h2 id="close-h" class="h2">pal runs on PAL Runtime.</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="margin:20px auto 0">The personal agent we use every day runs on this layer. Try the whole stack with Docker, no API key: the demo replays recorded model answers.</p>
      <Copy cmd={DEMO} label="Clone and start the demo" />
      <div class="actions"><a class="btn btn-rt" href={u('docs/runtime/quickstart/')}>Quickstart</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href={GH}>View on GitHub</a></div>
      <ol class="footnotes" style="margin:48px auto 0;text-align:left">
        <li id="fn-1"><span>1.</span><span>Without broker mode the allowlist is enforced inside the VM by a proxy, and secrets you vend to the workload do enter the guest.</span></li>
        <li id="fn-2"><span>2.</span><span>Enforced today: per-agent daily cost, runs and tokens, and a tenant-wide daily cost cap. Workflow runs also stop mid-run at a per-run cap and at per-tool limits.</span></li>
        <li id="fn-3"><span>3.</span><span>Policies are in place on every tenant table; enforcement is switched on per environment.</span></li>
        <li id="fn-4"><span>4.</span><span>Ed25519 once a signing key is configured; development falls back to HMAC.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
